   You mean Zork; Adventure (1975) didn't have the Great Underground Empire 
mythos behind it. Zork was written between 1977 and 1979, according to 
Wikipedia, but wasn't published until 1980.
   Nixon took the U.S. off the gold standard in August 1971, during the 
Vietnam War.

   According to Wikipedia (Gold as an investment#Gold price), gold was 
running $37.60 an ounce in the U.S. before the Nixon shock, and $641.20
in 1980. (Wow! Apparently gold was in short supply or great demand all
over; the record high price was $850 an ounce in January 1980.)

   So, if the writers of Zork were thinking of contemporary exchange rates, 
and not medieval rates (such as the D&D guidelines) or fantasy rates 
(i.e., totally fake and based on what feels right, or just ignoring rates 
altogether), then one gold zorkmid probably ought to be worth much /more/ 
than its price on the 2006 market.

